problems.problem:
$N$ friends simultaneously learned $N$ items of news, each of them learning one item. They began to phone each other and exchange news. Each conversation lasts $1$ hour. Any number of items of news can be passed on in one conversation. What is the minimal number of hours needed for everybody to learn all the news? Consider three cases in this problem:
a) $N=64$;
b) $N=55$;
c) $N=100$.

On a chessboard infinite in all directions some set $A$ of cells is selected. On all the cells of the board except those of the set $A$ there are kings. On a command all the kings can simultaneously make a move, which consists in a king either staying in place or occupying a neighbouring square, that is, making a "king's move". A king may also occupy the square that another king is leaving, but as a result of the move two kings may not end up in the same cell. Do there exist a $K$ and a way of moving the kings such that after $K$ moves the whole board is filled with kings? Consider the cases:
a) $A$ is the set of all cells both of whose coordinates are multiples of $100$ (it is assumed that one horizontal and one vertical line are numbered by all the integers from minus infinity to infinity, and each cell of the board is denoted by two numbers, its coordinates along these two axes);
b) $A$ is the set of all cells each of which is attacked by at least one of $100$ queens placed in some fixed way.

In Shvambrania there are $N$ cities, every two of which are joined by a road. The roads meet only in cities (there are no crossroads; one road is raised over another by an overpass). An evil wizard introduces one-way traffic on all the roads in such a way that if one can leave a city, then one cannot return to it. Prove that
a) the wizard can do this;
b) there is a city from which one can reach all the others, and there is a city from which one cannot leave;
c) the wizard can carry out his intention in $N!$ ways.

$n$ different cards are stacked in a deck. It is allowed to move any number of adjacent cards (without changing their order and without turning them over) to another place in the deck. It is required to put all $n$ cards in the reverse order by several such operations.
a) Prove that for $n=9$ this can be done in $5$ operations.
Prove that for $n=52$
b) this can be done in $27$ operations;
c) this cannot be done in $17$ operations;
d) this cannot be done in $26$ operations.

Does there exist a convex polyhedron one of whose cross-sections is a triangle (the section does not pass through vertices), and at each vertex of which there meet
a) at least five edges;
b) exactly five edges?

Can positive integers be placed in a $9\times 9$ table so that the following conditions hold simultaneously:
1) the products of the numbers standing in one row are the same for all rows;
2) the products of the numbers standing in one column are the same for all columns;
3) there are no equal numbers among them;
4) all the numbers are at most $1991$?

A "matloto" card is a $10\times 10$ table of cells. A player marks $10$ cells and sends the card in an envelope. After that ten losing cells are published in a newspaper. Prove that
a) one can fill in $13$ cards so that among them there will necessarily be a "winning" card, that is, one in which none of the losing cells is marked;
b) twelve cards are not enough for this.

In an isosceles triangle $ABC$ ($AB=AC$) the angle $A$ equals $\alpha$. A point $D$ is taken on the side $AB$ so that $AD=AB/n$. Find the sum of the $n-1$ angles under which the segment $AD$ is seen from the points dividing the side $BC$ into $n$ equal parts:
a) for $n=3$;
b) for arbitrary $n$.

Prove that there exist infinitely many triples of numbers $n-1$, $n$, $n+1$ such that:
a) $n$ can be represented as the sum of two squares of positive integers, while $n-1$ and $n+1$ cannot;
b) each of the three numbers can be represented as the sum of two squares of positive integers.

There is a set of $20$ weights with which one can weigh any integer weight from $1$ to $1997$ g (the weights are put on one pan of the balance, the weight being measured on the other). What is the minimal possible weight of the heaviest weight of such a set if:
a) the weights of the set are all integers;
b) the weights are not necessarily integers?

The function $f(x)=\frac{x^2+ax+b}{x^2+cx+d}$ is given, where the trinomials $x^2+ax+b$ and $x^2+cx+d$ have no common roots. Prove that the following two statements are equivalent:
1) there is a numerical interval free of values of the function;
2) $f(x)$ can be represented in the form $f(x)=f_1(f_2(\ldots f_{n-1}(f_n(x))\ldots))$, where each of the functions $f_i(x)$ is a function of one of the forms $k_ix+b_i$, $x^{-1}$, $x^2$.

The points $K$, $L$ on the sides $AC$, $CB$ of a triangle $ABC$ are the points at which the excircles touch these sides. Prove that the line joining the midpoints of $KL$ and $AB$
a) bisects the perimeter of triangle $ABC$;
b) is parallel to the bisector of the angle $ACB$.

Two circles meet at points $A$ and $B$. A line through the point $B$ meets the first and the second circles for the second time at points $K$ and $M$ respectively. The line $PQ$ is tangent to the first circle at the point $Q$ and is parallel to the line $AM$, and the line $PR$ is tangent to the second circle at the point $R$ and is parallel to the line $AK$. The points $Q$ and $R$ lie on different sides of the line $KM$. Prove that
a) the point $A$ lies on the line $QR$;
b) the point $P$ lies on the line $KM$.

$200$ points are given in space. Every two of them are joined by a segment, and the segments do not intersect each other. Each segment is coloured in one of $K$ colours. Petya wants to colour each point in one of the same colours so that there are no two points and a segment between them all coloured in the same colour. Can Petya always do this if
a) $K=7$;
b) $K=10$?

Let us say that a deck of $52$ cards is stacked correctly if every pair of adjacent cards agree in suit or in rank, the same holds for the top and the bottom card, and the ace of spades is on top. Prove that the number of ways to stack the deck correctly
a) is divisible by $12!$;
b) is divisible by $13!$.

$12$ grasshoppers sit at distinct points of a circle. These points divide the circle into $12$ arcs. At a signal the grasshoppers simultaneously jump clockwise, each from the endpoint of its arc to its midpoint. New $12$ arcs are formed, the jumps are repeated, and so on. Can at least one grasshopper return to its initial point after making
a) $12$ jumps;
b) $13$ jumps?

A paper triangle, one of whose angles equals $\alpha$, was cut into several triangles. Could it happen that all the angles of all the resulting triangles are less than $\alpha$
a) in the case $\alpha=70^\circ$;
b) in the case $\alpha=80^\circ$?

Two wizards fight each other. Initially both hover over the sea at a height of $100$ m. The wizards take turns casting spells of the form "decrease the height above the sea by $a$ m for oneself and by $b$ m for the opponent", where $a$, $b$ are real numbers with $0<a<b$. Both wizards have the same set of spells, which can be used in any order and repeatedly. A wizard wins the duel if after somebody's move his height above the sea is positive while his opponent's is not. Does there exist a set of spells such that the second wizard can guarantee a win (no matter how the first one acts), if the number of spells in the set is
a) finite;
b) infinite?

Does there exist a convex $N$-gon all of whose sides are equal and all of whose vertices lie on the parabola $y=x^2$, if
a) $N=2011$;
b) $N=2012$?

$N$ blue and $N$ red sticks are given, and the sum of the lengths of the blue sticks is equal to the sum of the lengths of the red ones. It is known that an $N$-gon can be formed from the blue sticks, and also from the red ones. Is it always possible to choose one blue and one red stick and recolour them (the blue one red and the red one blue) so that again an $N$-gon can be formed from the blue sticks and also from the red ones? Solve the problem
a) for $N=3$;
b) for an arbitrary positive integer $N$ greater than $3$.

Kostya had a pile of $100$ pebbles. In each move he split one of the piles into two smaller ones, until in the end he had $100$ piles of one pebble each. Prove that
a) at some moment some $30$ piles contained exactly $60$ pebbles in total;
b) at some moment some $20$ piles contained exactly $60$ pebbles in total;
c) Kostya could act so that at no moment were there $19$ piles containing exactly $60$ pebbles in total.

Several distinct real numbers are written on a blackboard. Peter wants to make an expression such that its values are exactly these numbers. To make such an expression, he may use any real numbers, brackets, and usual signs $+$, $-$ and $\times$. He may also use a special sign $\pm$: computing the values of the resulting expression, he chooses values $+$ or $-$ for every $\pm$ in all possible combinations. For instance, the expression $5\pm 1$ results in $\{4,6\}$, and $(2\pm 0.5)\pm 0.5$ results in $\{1,2,3\}$. Can Pete construct such an expression:
a) if the numbers on the blackboard are $1, 2, 4$;
b) for any collection of $100$ distinct real numbers on a blackboard?

Basil has a watermelon in a shape of a ball with diameter $20$ cm. Using a long knife, Basil makes three pairwise perpendicular cuts, each cut is of depth $h$ (a cut produces a circular segment with height $h$ in the plane of the cut). Does it necessarily follow that the watermelon is divided into two or more pieces if
a) $h=17$ cm;
b) $h=18$ cm?

A spherical planet has the equator of length $1$. On this planet, $N$ circular roads of length $1$ each are to be built and used for several trains each. The trains must have the same constant positive speed and never stop or collide. What is the greatest possible sum of lengths of all the trains? The trains are arcs of zero width with endpoints removed (so that if only endpoints of two arcs have coincided then it is not a collision). Solve the problem for
a) $N=3$;
b) $N=4$.

There are $100$ doors, each with its own key (which opens only this door). The doors are numbered $1$ through $100$, and so are the keys. It is known that the number of every key is either equal to the number of the door which it opens, or differs by $1$. For one attempt you can select any door and any key and check whether a chosen key opens a chosen door. Is it always possible to find out which key opens which door:
a) in $99$ attempts;
b) in $75$ attempts;
c) in $74$ attempts?

Prove that
a) any integer of the form $3k-2$, where $k$ is an integer, is the sum of a square and two cubes of some integers;
b) any integer is the sum of a square and three cubes of some integers.

Rockefeller and Marx play the following game. There are $n>1$ cities, each with the same number of citizens. At the start of the game every citizen has exactly one coin (all coins are identical). On his turn, Rockefeller chooses one citizen from every city, then Marx redistributes their coins between them so that the new distribution is different from one immediately before. Rockefeller wins if at some moment there will be at least one citizen in every city with no coin. Prove that Rockefeller can always win, no matter how Marx plays, if in every city there are
a) $2n$ citizens;
b) $2n-1$ citizens.

An equilateral triangle lying in the plane $\alpha$ is orthogonally projected onto a plane $\beta$, which is not parallel to $\alpha$. The resulting triangle is again orthogonally projected onto a plane $\gamma$, and it turned out that the image is again an equilateral triangle. Prove that
a) the angle between the planes $\alpha$ and $\beta$ is equal to the angle between the planes $\beta$ and $\gamma$;
b) the plane $\beta$ intersects the planes $\alpha$ and $\gamma$ along perpendicular lines.

The point $M$ inside a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ is equidistant from the lines $AB$ and $CD$ and is equidistant from the lines $BC$ and $AD$. The area of $ABCD$ occurred to be equal to $MA\cdot MC+MB\cdot MD$. Prove that the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is
a) tangential (circumscribed);
b) cyclic (inscribed).

A checkered square of size $2\times 2$ is covered by two triangles. Is it necessarily true that:
a) at least one of its four cells is fully covered by one of the triangles;
b) some square of size $1\times 1$ can be placed into one of these triangles?

An integer $n>2$ is given. Peter wants to draw $n$ arcs of length $\alpha$ of great circles on a unit sphere so that they do not intersect each other. Prove that
a) for all $\alpha<\pi+\frac{2\pi}{n}$ it is possible;
b) for all $\alpha>\pi+\frac{2\pi}{n}$ it is impossible.

It is known that among several banknotes of pairwise distinct face values (which are positive integers) there are exactly $N$ of false ones. In a single check, the detector determines the sum of the face values of all real banknotes in an arbitrary set selected by us. Prove that in $N$ checks all false banknotes can be detected, if
a) $N=2$;
b) $N=3$.

There are $N$ friends and a round pizza. It is allowed to make no more than $100$ straight cuts without shifting the slices until all cuts are done, then distribute all the resulting slices among all the friends so that each of them gets a share of pizza having the same summary area. Is there a cutting which gives the above result when
a) $N=201$;
b) $N=400$?

On a blank paper there were drawn two perpendicular axes $x$ and $y$ with the same scale. The graph of a function $y=f(x)$ was drawn in this coordinate system. Then the $y$ axis and all the scale marks on the $x$ axis were erased. Provide a way how to draw again the $y$ axis using pencil, ruler and compass if:
a) $f(x)=3^x$;
b) $f(x)=\log_a x$, where $a>1$ is an unknown number.

A starship is located in a halfspace at the distance $a$ from its boundary. The crew knows this but does not know which direction to move to reach the boundary plane. The starship may travel through the space by any path, may measure the way it has already travelled and has a sensor that signals when the boundary is reached. Is it possible to reach the boundary for sure, having passed no more than:
a) $14a$;
b) $13a$?

Alice and Bob are playing on a $100\times 100$ square table, each cell of which contains either a plus sign or a minus sign. They take turns crossing out rows and columns of the table: Alice on her turn can cross out any remaining row of the table; similarly, Bob on his turn can cross out any remaining column. They repeat that until only one cell remains. If that cell contains the plus sign, Alice wins; if the minus sign, Bob wins. Does there exist a table such that, no matter if Alice or Bob moves first, there is a winning strategy for
a) the second player;
b) the first player?

$N$ prisoners are held in cells placed in a circle. Tomorrow each prisoner will roll a die; after that, he would make a guess, predicting the numbers rolled by each of the $5$ prisoners in cells clockwise from his own. If at least one prisoner guesses at least one of the numbers correctly, all prisoners will be freed. Today, the prisoners can talk and decide on the common strategy, but after midnight, no communication will be possible. How should they act to guarantee their release if
a) $N=47$;
b) $N=48$?

Each side of a regular $N$-gon contains a robot. Each robot is moving along the side with constant speed that may be different for different robots; upon reaching the vertex, it turns around instantly and continues moving along the same side in the opposite direction. When two robots meet at a vertex, they produce a spark. Can it happen that at each vertex, the sparks appear with the same non-zero frequency if
a) $N=3$;
b) $N=5$?
